{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents adding, changing, or removing end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement already specifies in a Habeas project to draft requirements and commit them, once agreed under {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` and {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`, before writing implementation or test code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents that add, change, or remove end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement specifies to (a) draft requirements, (b) get them agreed, and (c) commit them before writing implementation or test code. The conditions of (b) and (c) are set in the cited `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` and `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`. Those are context and are not reassessed here. The `.commit` clause about leaving out unrelated edits belongs to that requirement.\n\n**Skill text.** I read `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. These are the workflow steps that matter:\n- Step 1 is the applicability check. It exempts changes with no observable behavior, or ones an existing requirement already specifies.\n- Step 2 is the draft step (\"Write drafts…\").\n- Step 3 is the review, which starts read-only subagents.\n- Step 4 holds the **Gate**.\n- Step 5 commits all groups together before any code.\n- Step 6 is \"Implement and test\".\n\n**Mapping obligations to tests.**\n- *Applicability scope (behavior that no requirement specifies; adding, changing, or removing).* `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]` asserts these:\n  - The step 1 phrases \"If the change adds, alters, or removes no observable behavior\" and \"an existing requirement already specifies it (most bug fixes), tell the user which requirement covers it\". They pin the scope and the exemption.\n  - The front-matter description contains \"before changing code, tests, or requirement text\", so the skill triggers before any change.\n  - The step containing \"Sort the new and changed requirements into groups\" is step 3.\n  - The phrase \"Every change to agreed text, including a rewording for precision or removing a requirement, goes through steps 3 to 5 before it is committed\". This covers changing and removing.\n- *Draft requirements, then commit them, before implementation.* `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code]` asserts the preamble that every end-user-observable result must follow from a user-agreed requirement. It also asserts the strict order `draft < review < gate < commit < implement`, using `step_of` on distinctive phrases in each step. `step_of` panics if a phrase is missing, so the assertions are enforced. The order covers drafting before commit, the gate before commit, and the commit (\"all groups together, before any code\") before \"Implement and test\".\n- *Agreement before commit or code.* The gate phrase is located by `step_of` in the test above. Its detailed obligation belongs to `.agreement` and its own test.\n\nI saw no uncovered obligation in the subject. Possible mutants, such as moving the commit after implementation, dropping the draft step, or changing the exemption wording, would each fail a phrase or ordering assertion. The test helpers (`step_of`, `assert_phrases`, `collapse`, `skill_md`, `description`) are simple lookups and substring checks. They are reached from marked tests, so they need no references of their own. There are no acknowledgments, and I found no gaps.",
  "references": "92bfb2508d227958b775e037320a82501c0b733b2fca5be678d89f5d5579945f",
